<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Why is there pus coming from my gums near a tooth?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Pus near a tooth is caused by a bacterial infection producing pus — almost always either a dental abscess (infection inside the tooth from decay reaching the pulp) or a periodontal abscess (infection in a gum pocket from advanced gum disease). The pus is the immune system's response to the bacteria, and the swelling develops as the pus collects in the gum tissue. <a href="dental-abscess-treatment-hyderabad.php" class="text-secondary underline">Dental abscess treatment</a> covers the dental cause in detail.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can I drain a gumboil at home?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">No — attempting to pop or drain a gumboil at home is dangerous and ineffective. Without source treatment, the abscess will refill. Additionally, introducing unsterile instruments into an oral infection can cause secondary infection. Gumboil drainage at CARE Hospitals Quality Hospitals is performed under sterile conditions, and the source tooth is treated simultaneously.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Will my gumboil come back?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Without treating the source tooth, a gumboil will always come back — sometimes within days of stopping antibiotics. Root canal therapy eliminates the source infection within the tooth and prevents recurrence. Extraction removes the source entirely. Our surgeons always discuss tooth-preservation options (root canal) before recommending extraction.</div>
</details>
</div>
